  "correct": "D",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use one trig function",
      "body": "<p>Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> in the given equation.</p>",
      "recap": "The Pythagorean identity reduces the equation.",
      "pitfall": "Do not replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.",
      "pause": "Simplify <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l16",
          "reminder": "Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> before solving in one trig function."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Keep both signs",
      "body": "<p>The reduced equation gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Squaring conceals the sign of sine.",
      "pitfall": "Taking only the positive root loses the largest angles.",
      "pause": "Which branch supplies solutions in the second half of the full turn?",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l16",
          "reminder": "Every square-root branch must be checked against the interval."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Locate every branch",
      "body": "<p>Use the unit circle to place all angles with the required positive or negative sine in the given full turn.</p>",
      "recap": "The sign and reference angle together locate a solution.",
      "pitfall": "The answer is in radians; check the interval endpoints.",
      "pause": "List the permitted angles and compare them.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l15",
          "reminder": "The sign and reference angle together locate a solution."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>sin</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p>On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, the solutions are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. The endpoints have sine zero, so they add no solutions.</p><p>The largest allowed angle is <strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>: D.</strong></p>",
